#!/bin/sh
#
# PRE-PUSH: the test suite runs before anything leaves this machine.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, written into the hook so it cannot be deleted as unexplained.
# On 13 August 2026 the integration branch was found with five failing tests that
# nobody had ever seen, because `npm test` had never been run on it. Two of them
# mattered a great deal:
#
#   * tests/unit/security/rls-column-exposure.test.ts had never executed AT ALL.
#     A shebang on the module it imports made the file fail to collect, so vitest
#     reported "no tests" and the suite passed vacuously. The guard for the
#     world-readable-column class was not running, and every gate stayed green.
#
#   * A merge-induced defect in the publish gate gave organisers a WRONG refusal
#     reason, because two correct changes on two branches did not compose. Both
#     branches passed their own tests; the composition was tested by nobody.
#
# Three of the five arrived AFTER the merges, on commits pushed straight to the
# integration branch. So the gap was not "run the suite before merging", it was
# "run the suite before pushing", which is what this hook does.
#
# WHY NOT IN THE GUARD RUNNER. `prebuild` runs the guards on every Vercel build,
# where they take seconds. The suite takes about fifty, and paying that on every
# deployment would buy nothing: the code being built has already been pushed. A
# push is the moment the work becomes shared, so a push is the right trigger.
#
# BYPASS. `git push --no-verify` skips this, and that is deliberate: a hook that
# cannot be bypassed in an emergency is a hook someone deletes. Bypassing it is a
# decision to push untested code, so make it knowingly.
#
# THE TYPECHECK IS HERE FOR A SEPARATE REASON, added 15 August 2026.
# On 15 August a type error reached the remote and put two preview deployments
# into ERROR. `tsc --noEmit` HAD been run and HAD exited 0, and was reported as
# evidence. It ran before the final edit and was quoted after it, so the claim
# was true when measured and false when made. That is the third instance of that
# shape in this repository (finding 79), after the stale node_modules junction
# and the Sensitive environment variable.
#
# A hook cannot stop somebody quoting an old measurement. It can make the LAST
# gate before code leaves the machine measure the tree that is actually leaving,
# which removes the window entirely. Vercel type-checks the whole tree including
# tests/, so a local `next build` does not catch what CI catches; `tsc --noEmit`
# against the repo tsconfig does.

echo "[pre-push] running the typecheck, lint and the test suite before this leaves the machine"

# TYPECHECK FIRST. It is the fastest of the three, so a type error fails in seconds
# rather than after a minute of tests.
npx tsc --noEmit
tsc_status=$?

if [ $tsc_status -ne 0 ]; then
  echo ""
  echo "[pre-push] BLOCKED: tsc --noEmit failed, so nothing was pushed."
  echo "[pre-push] Vercel type-checks tests/ as well as src/, so this is the same"
  echo "[pre-push] check that would have failed the preview build."
  exit $tsc_status
fi

echo "[pre-push] typecheck clean"

# LINT, ADDED 29 AUGUST 2026, because the hook and CI did not agree.
#
# `eslint --max-warnings=0` is the CI lint job. This hook ran the typecheck and
# the suite and NOT lint, so a push that satisfied the hook could still turn CI
# red, and every push looked clean from this machine while the job it maps to
# failed. On 28 August the integration branch was found with eight
# unused-variable warnings that had been red on CI since before that session
# began, and nobody saw them here.
#
# It is a WARNING-level failure that matters, which is exactly the kind a human
# scrolls past: --max-warnings=0 makes a warning a failure in CI, so the local
# gate has to use the same flag or it is measuring something softer than the
# thing it stands in for.
#
# It runs AFTER the typecheck and BEFORE the suite on purpose: slower than tsc,
# far faster than fifty seconds of tests, so the cheapest failures still come
# first.
# --cache IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE, and the reason is measured rather than assumed.
#
# The first version of this step ran a bare `eslint --max-warnings=0` and took
# OVER TWENTY MINUTES from the hook, on a tree where the same command run by
# hand finishes in a few. Two things make the hook's run the slow one: it uses
# whatever node is on PATH, which here is the system install rather than the
# pinned runtime, and it competes with whatever else the machine is doing at
# push time.
#
# A gate that turns every push into a twenty minute wait is a gate somebody
# starts passing --no-verify to, which is worse than not having it. The cache
# makes a repeat run proportional to what CHANGED rather than to the tree, so
# the first push after a cold checkout pays once and the rest are quick.
#
# .eslintcache is gitignored. It is a cache: deleting it costs one slow run and
# nothing else, and eslint invalidates entries by content hash, so a stale cache
# cannot hide a new warning.
npx eslint --max-warnings=0 --cache --cache-location .eslintcache
lint_status=$?

if [ $lint_status -ne 0 ]; then
  echo ""
  echo "[pre-push] BLOCKED: eslint --max-warnings=0 failed, so nothing was pushed."
  echo "[pre-push] This is the same command the CI lint job runs, so a push now"
  echo "[pre-push] would go red there. Warnings count: CI uses --max-warnings=0."
  exit $lint_status
fi

echo "[pre-push] lint clean"

# The CANARY runs the suite AND checks that nothing stopped running.
#
# A plain `npm test` was not enough. Twice a test file has reported healthy while
# executing nothing, and a green suite that runs fewer tests than yesterday looks
# identical to a green suite that runs all of them. The canary holds a committed
# floor for both the file count and the test count, so a silent drop is a red
# push rather than a number nobody compared.
#
# The repository pins its runtime in .nvmrc and package.json engines (Law 9).
# The hook does not choose a Node; it uses whatever is on PATH, and the guard
# suite reports whether that matches the contract.
node scripts/guards/test-count-canary.mjs
status=$?

if [ $status -ne 0 ]; then
  echo ""
  echo "[pre-push] BLOCKED: the test suite failed, so nothing was pushed."
  echo "[pre-push] Fix the failures, or push with --no-verify if you have decided"
  echo "[pre-push] to share untested code on purpose."
  exit $status
fi

echo "[pre-push] typecheck clean, lint clean, suite green, pushing"
exit 0
